WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.730
- [Narrator] This iconic dish
is all about the details.

00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:04.940
French toast might look
super simple to make,

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:06.630
but looks can be deceiving.

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:09.200
French toast is crispy on the outside,

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:11.210
that silky custard on the inside.

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:13.610
It's just so indulgent and delicious.

00:00:13.610 --> 00:00:14.830
What's not to love?

00:00:14.830 --> 00:00:16.360
So over the last couple of weeks,

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:19.740
we've eaten a ton of
butter and bread and syrup

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:23.125
to find out what makes the best
classic French Toast recipe.

00:00:23.125 --> 00:00:25.708
(upbeat music)

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:30.020
French toast is only as good as its bread.

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:31.440
So that's where we'll start.

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:33.030
There are a ton of options out there

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:35.480
and we tested a bunch of them.

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:36.400
For French toast,

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:39.000
we want to use a bread that's
both sturdy on the outside

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.350
and spongy and tender on the inside.

00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:44.510
Something like a brioche,
challah, or white bread.

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:48.130
We also tested milk bread,
Hawaiian bread, and sourdough

00:00:48.130 --> 00:00:50.440
but found they really
altered the flavor profile

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:51.370
of the dish.

00:00:51.370 --> 00:00:53.700
Ultimately, challah was our favorite.

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:56.010
We don't recommend
buying a pre-sliced loaf,

00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:58.970
because we want our slices
to be the perfect thickness.

00:00:58.970 --> 00:01:00.140
If they're too thin,

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:02.540
they'll fall apart with
the weight of the custard.

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:03.540
But if they're too thick,

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:05.880
it'll be difficult to cook the
custard all the way through

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:07.950
and nobody wants raw custard.

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:10.120
We think about an inch
to an inch and a half

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:11.800
is the perfect thickness.

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:14.440
Now this next part might
seem counterintuitive,

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:16.450
but we don't want to use fresh bread.

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:18.470
We want it to be at least a day old.

00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:21.820
Using fresh bread will result
in dense and soggy toast.

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:24.120
But don't worry if you
need French toast right now

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:25.460
and your bread isn't ready,

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:28.010
just bake your slices at
200 degrees Fahrenheit

00:01:28.010 --> 00:01:29.670
for 10 minutes to dry them out.

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:32.020
And then you'll be ready
to make your custard.

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:34.140
The custard is really
where the magic happens

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:35.810
when it comes to French toast.

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:38.300
The perfect custard
creates a crispy exterior

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:40.480
with a silky rich interior.

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:41.970
If you use too much milk,

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:43.700
or a lot of sugar in your custard,

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:46.850
the egg won't cook and you'll
get wet and soggy toast.

00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:48.220
So a good rule we follow

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:50.320
is that for about every
quarter cup of dairy,

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:51.930
we use one large egg.

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:54.710
For our recipe, we'll
be using six large eggs.

00:01:54.710 --> 00:01:56.640
It's important to
thoroughly whisk the eggs

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:58.410
so you don't end up with
clumps of egg whites

00:01:58.410 --> 00:01:59.243
in your custard.

00:01:59.243 --> 00:02:01.440
That will ruin an otherwise
perfectly golden brown

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:02.540
piece of toast.

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:05.010
Then we'll add our milk, heavy cream,

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:06.590
brown sugar, and salt.

00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:08.650
Finally, we're gonna add our seasoning.

00:02:08.650 --> 00:02:10.830
Seasoning your custard is crucial.

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:12.630
Now you can definitely get fancy with it

00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:16.090
by adding things like
cardamom, orange zest, nutmeg,

00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:18.250
cloves, ginger, lemon zest,

00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:19.720
or even liqueurs,

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:22.430
but we found that less is more
when it comes to seasoning,

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:25.110
so we're just adding a little cinnamon

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:26.490
and some vanilla paste.

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:29.130
You can totally make this
with vanilla extract,

00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:30.780
but we think it's worth the upgrade

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:32.260
to use vanilla bean paste.

00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:34.200
It just packs a bigger vanilla punch

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:36.230
and it's still cheaper than vanilla beans.

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:38.350
We'll whisk all of our
custard ingredients together

00:02:38.350 --> 00:02:39.720
until it's well combined.

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:42.360
Remember, we don't want any egg clumps.

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:43.610
Now that our custard's ready,

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:44.800
let's add our bread.

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:47.330
We want to make sure the custard
completely soaks the bread,

00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:49.110
but we don't want to soak it for so long

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:50.689
that it begins to disintegrate.

00:02:50.689 --> 00:02:52.780
Yeah, it's just super gross.

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:53.970
So we'll soak the bread

00:02:53.970 --> 00:02:55.940
until it begins to feel heavy in our hands

00:02:55.940 --> 00:02:57.930
and completely saturated throughout.

00:02:57.930 --> 00:03:00.260
This could mean anywhere from 20 seconds

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:02.090
to a minute on each side.

00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:05.020
Now that our bread has
taken a little custard bath,

00:03:05.020 --> 00:03:06.220
it's time to cook.

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:08.000
We tested several methods.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:10.830
Your cast iron has to
be seasoned super well

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:13.220
or your bread will end
up sticking to the pan.

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:15.750
The oven is a great method
for making big batches,

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:17.450
but doesn't get the crispy edges we want

00:03:17.450 --> 00:03:19.630
in a truly great French toast.

00:03:19.630 --> 00:03:22.770
So ultimately, we love using
a nonstick electric griddle.

00:03:22.770 --> 00:03:25.900
It distributes heat evenly
across its entire surface,

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:26.970
and it's also big enough

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:29.130
to cook large batches of toast at a time.

00:03:29.130 --> 00:03:30.490
But if you don't have a griddle,

00:03:30.490 --> 00:03:32.550
a nonstick pan works just as well.

00:03:32.550 --> 00:03:34.920
Now to get a golden brown crispy toast,

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:37.420
it's crucial to preheat
whatever pan your choose.

00:03:37.420 --> 00:03:38.550
By preheating the pan,

00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:40.740
the custard will begin
to cook immediately.

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:42.110
If the pan is cold from the start,

00:03:42.110 --> 00:03:44.190
the custard will spread to form a foot,

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:46.570
something like this, on
the bottom of the toast.

00:03:46.570 --> 00:03:48.330
We recommend preheating your pan

00:03:48.330 --> 00:03:50.370
before you start making your custard.

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:51.690
Once our pan is hot enough,

00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:54.268
we'll lightly grease
it with a pat of butter

00:03:54.268 --> 00:03:56.560
or something a little more than a pat.

00:03:56.560 --> 00:03:58.260
We like our butter here at Tasty.

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:00.060
Once all of that butter is melted,

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:02.220
we'll place the soaked toast on the pan

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:03.840
and let it cook undisturbed

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:05.530
until the bottom is golden brown,

00:04:05.530 --> 00:04:07.150
which takes about two minutes.

00:04:07.150 --> 00:04:09.640
Then we'll flip and
repeat on the other side.

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:11.160
Whoo, she's pretty!

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:14.040
Just look at how crispy
and golden brown that is.

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:16.530
Make sure to keep an eye
on your heat as you cook.

00:04:16.530 --> 00:04:18.770
If it's too high, the sugar
in the custard will burn

00:04:18.770 --> 00:04:20.400
before the custard is
cooked through all the way

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:21.233
in the middle.

00:04:21.233 --> 00:04:22.970
Once your toast is looking beautiful,

00:04:22.970 --> 00:04:24.630
we'll remove it from the pan.

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:25.900
And here's a little tip,

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:27.490
wipe your pan between batches

00:04:27.490 --> 00:04:29.600
to make sure the butter
doesn't continue to cook

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:32.220
and leave burnt patches
on the remaining toast.

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:35.180
Now that we've whipped up perfect
golden brown crispy toast,

00:04:35.180 --> 00:04:36.200
it's time to serve.

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:37.610
You can really get creative

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:38.970
with the toppings for this.

00:04:38.970 --> 00:04:43.010
Use whipped cream, chocolate
syrup, sprinkles, bananas,

00:04:43.010 --> 00:04:44.700
you can go wild.

00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:46.930
But we think that less is more.

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:48.985
We love to serve ours with a pat of butter

00:04:48.985 --> 00:04:51.030
and some maple syrup.

00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:52.380
Did you know that there's a difference

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:55.180
between pure maple
syrup and pancake syrup?

00:04:55.180 --> 00:04:56.810
Pancake syrup is actually made

00:04:56.810 --> 00:04:58.830
with corn syrup and maple flavoring.

00:04:58.830 --> 00:05:00.550
It's definitely a more affordable

00:05:00.550 --> 00:05:02.100
and shelf stable alternative.

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:03.220
But if you can splurge,

00:05:03.220 --> 00:05:06.290
we love using warm, pure maple syrup.

00:05:06.290 --> 00:05:08.250
Drizzle that right over
the top of your toast.

00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:10.060
And if you want to get fancy with it,

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:12.140
finish with a little flaky sea salt.

00:05:12.140 --> 00:05:14.380
The sea salt really
complements the sweetness

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:16.000
in the custard and syrup.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:17.550
We like to add a little pop of color.

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:19.740
So we'll serve ours with a mix of berries.

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:21.470
We're just using our favorites here.

00:05:21.470 --> 00:05:22.590
And we're done.

00:05:22.590 --> 00:05:24.950
All that's left to do now
is enjoy your work of art

00:05:24.950 --> 00:05:26.270
with your friends and your family

00:05:26.270 --> 00:05:28.470
on a wonderful brunch afternoon

00:05:28.470 --> 00:05:30.610
or just indulge by yourself.

00:05:30.610 --> 00:05:33.220
Either way, if you loaf this recipe,

00:05:33.220 --> 00:05:36.490
you can find it and many more
on our website at tasty.co.

00:05:36.490 --> 00:05:38.520
What's that thing that
you put on French toast?

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:39.353
- [Second Narrator] Syrup.

00:05:39.353 --> 00:05:40.970
- [Narrator] Are you saying sir-up?

00:05:40.970 --> 00:05:41.910
- [Second Narrator] How do you say it?

00:05:41.910 --> 00:05:43.550
- [Narrator] Bro, it's seer-up.

00:05:43.550 --> 00:05:45.723
- [Second Narrator] I think
people say it like me.

